---
import InnerLayout from '../../layouts/InnerLayout.astro';
import ArchitectureDiagram from '../../components/ArchitectureDiagram.astro';
import ClaudePipeline from '../../components/ClaudePipeline.astro';

const stats = [
  { n: '3', label: 'client apps on one API', sub: 'web, phone, and a Go CLI' },
  { n: '81', label: 'in-app help articles', sub: 'written alongside the features' },
  { n: '9', label: 'Claude Code skills', sub: 'plus developer and UAT agents' },
  { n: '4', label: 'test layers', sub: 'PHPUnit, Vitest, Go, Playwright' },
];

const decisions = [
  {
    k: 'Scheduling',
    title: 'A series is stored once, and its dates are computed',
    body: 'A recurring job is stored as one record with a recurrence rule. The calendar calculates each date from that rule when someone opens it, and a date becomes its own record only when someone changes, confirms, or invoices it. I chose this design so that a weekly client does not turn into hundreds of rows, and so that an edit to the series reaches every date that has not already been changed by itself. Recurrence is the most complex logic in the app, especially where timezones and daylight saving time are involved, so I gave it the most thorough test coverage.',
  },
  {
    k: 'Identity',
    title: 'Keycloak handles sign-in, so the API only checks tokens',
    body: 'I did not want to build password storage, passkeys, social sign-in, and session handling myself, so I chose Keycloak, which provides all of them. The web app, the phone app, and the CLI share one OpenID Connect sign-in flow. The API validates the token on every request and limits the data to the user’s organization, and the role model decides what an owner, a dispatcher, and a technician can each see.',
  },
  {
    k: 'Data',
    title: 'Nothing disappears',
    body: 'Records are soft-deleted, changes to settings are written to an audit log, and an issued invoice has a locked total. I designed it this way so that when a client disputes what was agreed, the owner can see what changed, when it changed, and who changed it. Every resource also uses a typed, prefixed identifier, so an ID that appears in a log or a support message shows what kind of record it is.',
  },
  {
    k: 'Messaging',
    title: 'Client messages go through a queue and a set of rules',
    body: 'The API never sends a message while it is handling a request. Instead, it places the message on a RabbitMQ queue, and a worker checks the delivery rules before sending it. The rules cover quiet hours, a daily limit for each client, the owner’s send window, and fixed contact hours in the client’s own timezone. When a message is not allowed yet, the worker holds it until it is allowed instead of dropping it.',
  },
  {
    k: 'Money',
    title: 'Daybourne drafts, and the owner issues',
    body: 'Daybourne drafts invoices automatically on the billing day, but a person always issues them. The first beta tester keeps their books in QuickBooks Online, so I planned the QuickBooks sync around the same rule. Daybourne will draft the invoice, QuickBooks will issue it, and no invoice or payment will be entered twice.',
  },
  {
    k: 'Testing',
    title: 'Functional tests against a real database',
    body: 'I prefer functional tests that run against a real container and database over unit tests that rely on mocks. Each test layer covers only what it can see. PHPUnit covers the HTTP contract and permissions, Vitest covers component logic, Playwright covers user flows across pages, and the Go suite runs the CLI against the live API.',
  },
];

const guardrails = [
  { t: 'Shared context', d: 'Each component has its own CLAUDE.md, and the product context and standing decisions live in two short documents that every planning skill reads first. Agents do not re-ask questions I have already answered.' },
  { t: 'Path-scoped rules', d: 'Rules for the schema, recurrence, notifications, observability, and help docs load automatically when an agent touches files in those areas.' },
  { t: 'Hooks', d: 'A hook type-checks client files after every edit. At the end of each turn, a second hook runs the compile and style checks (PHPStan and the TypeScript compiler) on whatever changed.' },
  { t: 'Specialist skills', d: '/api-endpoint scaffolds or reviews an API Platform endpoint against the repository’s patterns, /verify drives the live stack with curl and Playwright, and /daybourne-design applies the design system.' },
  { t: 'Bounded loops', d: 'The review gate gets two passes and UAT gets three cycles. If problems keep coming back, the branch comes to me instead of looping.' },
  { t: 'Documentation as done', d: 'A user-facing change is not finished until the in-app help describes it. A check script fails the branch when a screen changed but its guide did not.' },
];
---
<InnerLayout
  title="Daybourne case study"
  activePage="portfolio"
  description="How Michael Bos is building Daybourne, a recurring-service management platform, with Symfony, React, Keycloak, and a pipeline of Claude Code agents."
>
  <style is:global>
    .cs { max-width: 1180px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 24px; }
    .cs-crumb {
      margin-top: 32px;
      font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--fs-11); letter-spacing: var(--tr-caps); text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--canyon-fade);
    }
    .cs-crumb a { color: var(--rust); text-decoration: none; }
    .cs-crumb a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
    .cs-hero { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.15fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 48px; align-items: center; margin-top: 18px; }
    .cs-id { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px; }
    .cs-id img { width: 40px; height: 44px; }
    .cs-id .tag {
      font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--fs-11); letter-spacing: var(--tr-caps); text-transform: uppercase; color: #6E7E4D;
    }
    .cs-hero h1 {
      margin: 0 0 18px; font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 56px; font-weight: 500;
      letter-spacing: -0.03em; line-height: 1; color: var(--canyon-ink);
    }
    .cs-lede { margin: 0 0 14px; font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--canyon-shadow); text-wrap: pretty; max-width: 60ch; }
    .cs-shot {
      position: relative; border-radius: 12px; overflow: hidden;
      border: 1px solid rgba(92, 107, 62, 0.25);
      box-shadow: 0 30px 60px -30px rgba(31, 20, 12, 0.45);
      transform: perspective(1400px) rotateY(-8deg) rotateX(3deg);
      transition: transform 600ms var(--ease-out);
    }
    .cs-shot:hover { transform: none; }
    .cs-shot img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }

    .cs-stats { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 16px; margin-top: 56px; }
    .cs-stat { padding: 18px 20px; border-radius: var(--r-lg); background: var(--card-bg); border: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .cs-stat .n { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 40px; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -0.03em; color: var(--rust); line-height: 1; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
    .cs-stat .l { margin-top: 8px; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; color: var(--canyon-ink); }
    .cs-stat .s { margin-top: 2px; font-size: 13px; color: var(--canyon-fade); }

    .cs-sec { margin-top: 88px; }
    .cs-sec-head { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 220px minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 32px; margin-bottom: 28px; }
    .cs-sec-head .num {
      font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--fs-12); letter-spacing: var(--tr-caps); text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--rust);
      padding-top: 10px;
    }
    .cs-sec-head h2 { margin: 0 0 12px; font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 34px; font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: -0.025em; color: var(--canyon-ink); line-height: 1.1; }
    .cs-sec-head p { margin: 0 0 10px; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--canyon-shadow); max-width: 70ch; text-wrap: pretty; }

    .cs-decisions { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 16px; }
    .cs-dec {
      padding: 22px 24px; border-radius: var(--r-lg); background: var(--card-bg); border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
      transition: border-color 200ms, transform 200ms var(--ease-out), box-shadow 200ms;
    }
    .cs-dec:hover { border-color: rgba(184, 74, 38, 0.35); transform: translateY(-2px); box-shadow: var(--shadow-2); }
    .cs-dec .k { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: var(--tr-caps); text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--rust); }
    .cs-dec h3 { margin: 6px 0 10px; font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 18px; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -0.01em; color: var(--canyon-ink); }
    .cs-dec p { margin: 0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--canyon-shadow); text-wrap: pretty; }

    .cs-guard { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 1px; background: var(--hairline); border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: var(--r-lg); overflow: hidden; margin-top: 32px; }
    .cs-guard > div { background: var(--bg-bone); padding: 20px 22px; }
    .cs-guard h3 { margin: 0 0 8px; font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--canyon-ink); }
    .cs-guard p { margin: 0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--canyon-shadow); text-wrap: pretty; }

    .cs-media { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 32px; align-items: center; margin-top: 40px; }
    .cs-media p { margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--canyon-shadow); text-wrap: pretty; }
    .cs-media .frame { border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid var(--hairline-strong); box-shadow: var(--shadow-2); }
    .cs-media .frame img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }

    .cs-next {
      margin-top: 88px; padding: 32px 36px; border-radius: var(--r-lg);
      background: #F4F5EC; border: 1px solid rgba(92, 107, 62, 0.25);
      display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto; gap: 24px; align-items: center;
    }
    .cs-next h2 { margin: 0 0 8px; font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 22px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--canyon-ink); }
    .cs-next p { margin: 0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--canyon-shadow); max-width: 70ch; }
    .cs-back {
      display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; height: 38px; padding: 0 16px; border-radius: var(--r-pill);
      background: #5C6B3E; color: #F4F5EC; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; white-space: nowrap;
    }
    .cs-back:hover { background: #4C5933; color: #F4F5EC; text-decoration: none; }

    /* Sections rise in as they scroll into view, where the browser supports it */
    @supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
        .cs-rise {
          animation: cs-rise linear both;
          animation-timeline: view();
          animation-range: entry 0% entry 40%;
        }
        @keyframes cs-rise { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(24px); } to { opacity: 1; transform: none; } }
      }
    }

    @media (max-width: 960px) {
      .cs-hero { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 28px; }
      .cs-hero h1 { font-size: 42px; }
      .cs-shot { transform: none; }
      .cs-stats { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
      .cs-sec-head { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 4px; }
      .cs-sec-head .num { padding-top: 0; }
      .cs-decisions, .cs-media { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
      .cs-guard { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
      .cs-next { grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding: 24px; }
    }
  </style>

  <article class="cs">
    <div class="cs-crumb"><a href="/portfolio">Portfolio</a> / Case study</div>

    <header class="cs-hero">
      <div>
        <div class="cs-id">
          <img src="/daybourne-mark.svg" alt="" />
          <span class="tag">Side project · In progress</span>
        </div>
        <h1>Daybourne</h1>
        <p class="cs-lede">
          Daybourne is a recurring-service management platform for owner-operators with
          one to three people whose revenue comes mostly from repeat clients on a schedule.
          The first beta tester runs a solo pool service with a weekly route, sends one
          invoice per client each month, and keeps the books in QuickBooks Online.
        </p>
        <p class="cs-lede">
          Most owners in this group run their business from a calendar app, text
          messages, and a spreadsheet. The field service tools on the market are built for
          dispatch offices and one-off jobs, so they ask a solo owner to do a lot of setup
          for features they will not use. I set out to build something smaller that
          handles the whole weekly loop of visits, messages, invoices, and expenses.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-shot">
        <img src="/portfolio/daybourne/today.webp" alt="The Today page in Daybourne, showing the day's progress, the next visits, and the timeline" />
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="cs-stats cs-rise">
      {stats.map(s => (
        <div class="cs-stat">
          <div class="n">{s.n}</div>
          <div class="l">{s.label}</div>
          <div class="s">{s.sub}</div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <section class="cs-sec" id="architecture" aria-labelledby="arch-h">
      <div class="cs-sec-head cs-rise">
        <div class="num">01 · Architecture</div>
        <div>
          <h2 id="arch-h">How the pieces fit together</h2>
          <p>
            The backend is a Symfony and API Platform application running on FrankenPHP,
            with PostgreSQL for data, RabbitMQ for background work, and S3-compatible
            storage for receipt photos. Keycloak handles identity for every client. The web
            app is a React single-page app in a Turborepo monorepo that also holds the
            mobile app and the shared API client and types. The whole stack runs locally
            under Docker Compose, including Grafana for logs, traces, and metrics.
          </p>
          <p>Pick a walkthrough to follow one request through the system.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <ArchitectureDiagram />
    </section>

    <section class="cs-sec" id="decisions" aria-labelledby="dec-h">
      <div class="cs-sec-head cs-rise">
        <div class="num">02 · Decisions</div>
        <div>
          <h2 id="dec-h">Decisions that shaped it</h2>
          <p>
            Each of these is written down in the repository as an architecture document or a
            standing product decision, so the agents that build features follow them too.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-decisions">
        {decisions.map(d => (
          <div class="cs-dec cs-rise">
            <span class="k">{d.k}</span>
            <h3>{d.title}</h3>
            <p>{d.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cs-sec" id="process" aria-labelledby="ai-h">
      <div class="cs-sec-head cs-rise">
        <div class="num">03 · Process</div>
        <div>
          <h2 id="ai-h">How I build it with Claude Code</h2>
          <p>
            I build Daybourne by myself, so I set it up the way I would set up a team. Each
            role in a feature's life is a Claude Code skill with a written procedure: a
            product owner, an architect, a tech lead, a project manager, and a dev team lead
            who runs developer agents in parallel. Every stage produces a document that the
            next stage reads, and the stages that decide what gets built can only be started
            by me.
          </p>
          <p>
            My part is close to my role at work. I make the product decisions, approve the
            requirements and the plan, and review what comes back before it merges.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ClaudePipeline />

      <div class="cs-guard cs-rise">
        {guardrails.map(g => (
          <div>
            <h3>{g.t}</h3>
            <p>{g.d}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="cs-media cs-rise">
        <div>
          <p>
            The help center is part of that definition of done. Every screen has a guide that
            opens beside the work, and there are 81 articles today. The screenshots and short
            clips in those guides are captured by a Playwright suite that signs in to a seeded
            demo business at a fixed date and time, so a recapture produces the same picture
            every time.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every image and clip of the app on this site came from that suite. I cropped them
            and did not stage anything by hand.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <img src="/portfolio/daybourne/help.webp" alt="The help panel open beside the invoicing settings, explaining the billing day" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cs-next cs-rise">
      <div>
        <h2>What comes next</h2>
        <p>
          The next milestone is a small, free beta for pool service owners and music
          teachers, who will request access to join. Before that, I need to finish the QuickBooks Online sync, form
          the business, publish the terms and privacy pages, and put the app online.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a class="cs-back" href="/portfolio">
        <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 12H5M12 19l-7-7 7-7"/></svg>
        Back to the feature tour
      </a>
    </section>
  </article>
</InnerLayout>
